Objective

Learn the fundamentals of using Splunk, including how to search, analyze, and visualize log data to support security monitoring and incident investigation in a SOC environment.

Step 1: Introduction to Splunk

The room introduced Splunk as a powerful platform used for searching, monitoring, and analyzing machine-generated data.

Key use cases:

Log analysis
Security monitoring (SIEM)
Incident investigation
Operational intelligence

Splunk allows analysts to quickly turn raw log data into actionable insights.

Step 2: Understanding Splunk Interface

The Splunk interface provides several key areas for analysts:

Main components:

Search & Reporting app – used to query and analyze data
Search bar – where queries are written using SPL
Time range selector – filters events by time
Fields panel – shows extracted fields from events
Events viewer – displays raw log entries

Understanding the interface is essential for efficient investigation.

Step 3: Basic Searches (SPL)

Splunk uses Search Processing Language (SPL) to query data.

Basic search examples:

Search all data in an index

index=main

Filter by keyword

index=main error

Filter by specific field

index=main status=404

These searches help analysts quickly locate relevant events.

Step 4: Working with Fields

Fields are key-value pairs extracted from log data.

Common fields:

host – source machine
source – origin of the log
sourcetype – type of data
status, user, ip – event-specific details

Example:

index=main user=admin

Fields make it easier to filter and analyze large datasets.

Step 5: Using Search Commands

Splunk provides commands to refine and analyze results.

Common commands:

stats – perform calculations

index=main | stats count by host

sort – order results

index=main | sort -count

table – display selected fields

index=main | table host, status, user

These commands help transform raw data into meaningful insights.

Step 6: Visualization

Splunk allows analysts to visualize data for better understanding.

Visualization types:

Bar charts
Line graphs
Pie charts
Tables

Visualizations help identify trends, anomalies, and patterns quickly.

Step 7: Outcome

By completing this room, I gained hands-on experience using Splunk to search, filter, and analyze log data, which is essential for SOC analysts performing threat detection and investigation.

